   // common parameters
   "common_param_1" : "common value 1",
   "common_param_2" : "common value 2"
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
   // common parameters
   "[import]" : "./common.jsonp",
   //
   // component A parameters
   "componentA_param_1" : "componentA value 1",
   "componentA_param_2" : "componentA value 2"
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
   // common parameters
   "[import]" : "./common.jsonp",
   //
   // component B parameters
   "componentB_param_1" : "componentB value 1",
   "componentB_param_2" : "componentB value 2"
}
</code></pre> <p>JSON files are imported with the key <code>"[import]"</code>. The value of this key is the path and name of the JSON file to be imported.</p> <ul> <li>A relative path is relative to the location of the importing file.</li> <li>A JSON file can contain more than one import.</li> <li>Imports can be nested: an imported JSON file can import further JSON files.</li> </ul> <p>Outcome of <code>componentA.jsonp</code>:</p> <pre><code>{'common_param_1': 'common value 1',
 'common_param_2': 'common value 2',
 'componentA_param_1': 'componentA value 1',
 'componentA_param_2': 'componentA value 2'}
</code></pre> <p>Outcome of <code>componentB.jsonp</code>:</p> <pre><code>{'common_param_1': 'common value 1',
 'common_param_2': 'common value 2',
 'componentB_param_1': 'componentB value 1',
 'componentB_param_2': 'componentB value 2'}
</code></pre> <p>The returned dictionary contains both the parameters from the loaded JSON file and the parameters imported by the loaded JSON file.</p> <p>How an import works</p> <p>You can imagine an import as follows: the <code>"[import]"</code> line is replaced by the content (the key-value pairs) of the imported file - at exactly this position. This is the reason why the position of an import matters, see Overwriting parameters.</p>
